<!-- ============ ONE PLATFORM VS THE PLUGIN STACK (shared) ============ -->
<section class="section fstack-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Built In, Not Bolted On</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">The plugin stack stops scaling before you do.</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Every capability a growing brand adds through an app is another vendor, another contract, another data model, and another thing to re-test on release day. That math gets worse with every store and every market.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="fstack">
      <div class="fstack-col fstack-them">
        <div class="fstack-head">
          <span class="fstack-tag">The assembled stack</span>
          <h3>One app per problem.</h3>
        </div>
        <ul class="fstack-list">
          <li><i class="ti ti-plug"></i> A clienteling app, bought and integrated separately</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-plug"></i> An RFID layer added through custom extensions</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-plug"></i> A PIM for product data, syncing on a schedule</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-plug"></i> A CDP for customer data, syncing on another</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-plug"></i> Order management, loyalty, and training as three more vendors</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-plug"></i> Fiscal compliance handled market by market, locally</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="fstack-foot fstack-foot-them">
          <i class="ti ti-alert-triangle"></i>
          <p>Each integration is a seam. Seams are where data disagrees, releases break, and the cost of running the estate quietly compounds.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="fstack-vs"><span>vs</span></div>

      <div class="fstack-col fstack-us">
        <div class="fstack-head">
          <span class="fstack-tag fstack-tag-us">Teamwork Commerce</span>
          <h3>One codebase, ten capability areas.</h3>
        </div>
        <ul class="fstack-list">
          <li><i class="ti ti-check"></i> <a href="/features/customer-data/">Customer data and clienteling</a>, native</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-check"></i> <a href="/platform/rfid/">End-to-end RFID</a>, no middleware, native</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-check"></i> <a href="/features/product-data/">Product information management</a>, native</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-check"></i> <a href="/platform/oms/">Order management and fulfillment</a>, native</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-check"></i> <a href="/features/training/">Associate training</a> and <a href="/features/access-identity/">identity</a>, native</li>
          <li><i class="ti ti-check"></i> Fiscal compliance in 40+ countries. Built in</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="fstack-foot fstack-foot-us">
          <i class="ti ti-circle-check-filled"></i>
          <p>One version of the truth across every store and channel, and one upgrade path. Extend it with your own services through open APIs when you need to, without forking the POS.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="fstack-note">A stack assembled from apps works until store count, market count, and integration count start multiplying at the same time. That is usually the moment a growing brand discovers what its architecture actually costs.</p>
  </div>
</section>
